{
  "id": "ligths___color_temperatures",
  "title": "Ligths - Color Temperatures",
  "description": "Light sources and their color irradiation in degrees Kelvin. The Correlated Color Temperature (CCT) is the absolute temperature of a black radiating reference light source which emits the same color irradiation as a given light source measured in degrees Kelvin (K).",
  "type": "calculator-table",
  "components": [
    {
      "type": "markdown",
      "content": "## Color Temperature Classifications\n\n* **Cold colors** (bluish white): > 5300 K\n* **Neutral colors**: 3300 - 5300 K\n* **Warm colors** (yellowish white through red): < 3300 K"
    },
    {
      "type": "calculator",
      "inputs": [
        {
          "id": "cct_value",
          "label": "Color Temperature",
          "type": "number",
          "unit": "K",
          "defaultValue": 6500
        }
      ],
      "outputs": [
        {
          "id": "color_classification",
          "label": "Color Classification",
          "formula": "inputs.cct_value > 5300 ? 'Cold (Bluish White)' : inputs.cct_value >= 3300 ? 'Neutral' : 'Warm (Yellowish-Red)'"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "datatable",
      "headers": [
        "Light Source",
        "Correlated Color Temperature (K)"
      ],
